          "question": "The right corner of my mouth has stopped moving and the arm on that side feels heavy since I woke up.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now. Do not wait to see if this passes.** A sudden droop on one side of the face with a heavy arm on the same side is a possible stroke, and it must be treated as one until a clinician rules it out. Time matters a great deal, so do not drive yourself and do not wait for a clinic appointment.\n\n**Note the time.** The symptoms began on waking, so the time to report is when you last knew you were well, usually when you went to sleep. Tell the emergency team that time, and tell them your symptoms started on waking.\n\nNote on the triage tool: its result for this description was \"routine,\" with no red-flag match. That does not match the guidance I retrieved. The stroke guidance (red-flag-stroke-symptoms) says sudden one-sided face drooping and sudden arm weakness are treated as a stroke until proven otherwise, and that these need emergency services immediately. I am following that guidance, not the \"routine\" label.\n\n**What the findings involve, grouped by body system:**\n- **Nervous system (face):** one-sided drooping of the mouth, which the stroke guidance lists as a stroke warning sign.\n- **Nervous system (arm):** heavy weakness in one arm, also listed in the same guidance.\n\nBoth findings are on the same side of the body and began suddenly, which is the pattern the guidance names. Other causes can exist, but only a clinician can assess them, and they should be looked at urgently in the same way.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
